Original commit body from fbe642b940bd54f1f303e7663188793f187750c7:

Two verified defects, both reproduced with canaries before and after the fix.
Both are preconditions for handing hypergumbo to anyone else to run.

1. `cache-clear --repo <path>` DELETED THAT PATH.

   `cache_dir / repo` discards the left operand when `repo` is absolute, and
   `..` traverses out; the only downstream check was `is_dir()`, which tests
   existence rather than containment. `--repo` is the same flag name every
   other subcommand uses for a path, so `cache-clear --repo "$REPO"` in a
   script deleted the working tree. Reproduced: a directory outside the cache
   with files in it, removed, reported as `Deleted repo ... (36.0 B)`.

   The structural fix is that safety_zones wrappers now ENFORCE the zone they
   declare. `_safety_zone_barrier()` was an empty function and every wrapper
   was a docstring claiming a boundary it did not check. All three destructive
   wrappers (cache_rmtree, tmp_artifact_rmtree, install_artifact_unlink) now
   refuse an out-of-zone path, resolving symlinks first so `<cache>/evil -> /`
   cannot slip through. A PARITY TEST enumerates every *_rmtree/*_unlink and
   fails if one does not enforce, so the next wrapper cannot ship unguarded --
   the discipline existed only in prose, and prose does not fail a build.
   The CLI additionally refuses a path-shaped `--repo` at the boundary with a
   usable message and exit 2, enumerating what is PERMITTED (one plain
   component) rather than blocklisting separator spellings.

2. ANALYSING A REPO EXECUTED CODE FROM THAT REPO.

   Runtime subcommands shelled out to `git` with cwd inside the target.
   `hypergumbo io-boundaries <hostile-repo>` executed an attacker-supplied
   program 6 times, as the invoking user, exit 0, silently. Three independent
   vectors were demonstrated on `git status` alone:
     - core.fsmonitor -- a program path in the repo's own .git/config;
     - .git/hooks/post-index-change -- fires with NO config keys set, so
       auditing .git/config is not a mitigation;
     - filter.<driver>.clean armed by an in-tree .gitattributes -- measured to
       survive core.fsmonitor=false + core.hooksPath=/dev/null +
       core.attributesFile=/dev/null + --literal-pathspecs +
       --no-optional-locks applied TOGETHER. The attacker names the driver,
       so no hardening list closes it.

   Hardening therefore cannot work, and a flag list would rot silently as git
   gains exec-capable keys. The fix is to not run `git status`: its result was
   never load-bearing, feeding only a cache-key/provenance digest. The
   fingerprint now hashes the working tree directly, which is strictly more
   precise. Original exploit re-run against the fix: 6 executions -> 0.

   COST, measured rather than assumed: 0.01s -> 0.12s (pretix) / 1.7s
   (hypergumbo), once per analysis, against runs that take minutes. Two
   consequences, both measured: non-analysed files (.txt/.md) no longer
   invalidate the cache, which is correct and means FEWER cold runs; and
   gitignored source files now count -- 2 on hypergumbo, 0 on pretix, 0 on
   kserve, so the extra invalidation is negligible. Two tests asserting the
   old over-invalidating behaviour via a .txt file were updated to use an
   analysed file, and the companion property (a non-analysed file must NOT
   bust the cache) is now pinned explicitly.

KNOWN GAP FOUND AND DELIBERATELY NOT FIXED HERE: `_SOURCE_EXTENSIONS` names
no .yaml/.toml/.json/.xml/Dockerfile though hypergumbo analyses all of them,
so editing a pyproject.toml serves a stale analysis -- under-invalidation,
which predates this change. Deriving the set from `taxonomy.get_language` was
tried and REVERTED: .json is both an analysed language and the extension of
hypergumbo's own output, so it re-pollutes the cache key every run. It needs
artifact-name exclusion, a cache-semantics change that has no business riding
along with a security fix. Recorded in the source at the point of the defect.

STILL OPEN, disclosed rather than silently carried: `rust-analyzer scip`
executes a target project's build.rs (canary-verified). Verified fixable --
`--config-path` with cargo.buildScripts and procMacro disabled produced an
index with the canary silent -- but that is a separate change.

